---
import CompareLayout from "../../layouts/CompareLayout.astro";
import { deleteField } from "../../data/demo-answers.ts";
import { SFI, VERIFIED } from "../../data/compare.ts";
---

<CompareLayout
  name="Elements.cloud"
  title="sf-intelligence vs Elements.cloud: MCP, Price, Data"
  description="Elements.cloud now ships a metadata MCP server. Compare it with free, local sf-intelligence on price, where your metadata lives, and who each one is for."
  path="/compare/elements-cloud"
  heading="sf-intelligence vs Elements.cloud"
  lede="Both now answer “what breaks if I change this?” over MCP, inside Claude Code, Cursor and Copilot. The differences that remain are price, where your metadata is processed, and how much of a platform you want."
  short="Elements.cloud is a commercial metadata-management platform with a hosted metadata graph and an MCP server for coding agents. Its Core plan starts at $6,800 a year for 100 Salesforce users. sf-intelligence is free, runs on your computer, and keeps the metadata there. Pick Elements for a team-wide governance platform with a vendor behind it; pick sf-intelligence to ask the same class of questions for $0 without uploading anything."
  rows={[
    { label: "What it is", sfi: SFI.what, them: "A metadata management platform: a metadata graph of the org, change intelligence and process mapping" },
    { label: "MCP server", sfi: "Yes. It is an MCP server", them: "Yes. Their site lists Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot and Agentforce as clients" },
    { label: "Where metadata is processed", sfi: SFI.where, them: "In Elements’ service" },
    { label: "Writes to the org", sfi: SFI.writes, them: "Check with Elements for the actions its agents can take" },
    { label: "Price", sfi: SFI.price, them: "Core Metadata Management from $6,800 a year for 100 Salesforce users ($21,120 for 500, $29,440 for 1,000). Advanced plan and add-ons quoted by sales" },
    { label: "Free option", sfi: "Everything is free", them: "No free plan listed. Trials are set up with their team" },
    { label: "Source code", sfi: SFI.source, them: "Closed" },
    { label: "Built for", sfi: "Admins, developers and architects asking questions in their AI assistant", them: "Teams running Salesforce change governance and process work across the org" },
  ]}
  chooseSfi={[
    "Answers about your org in your AI assistant <strong>without paying per Salesforce user</strong>",
    "Your org’s metadata to <strong>stay on your own computer</strong>",
    "To read the code that produces each answer",
    "To try it on a demo org today, with no sales call",
  ]}
  chooseThem={[
    "A <strong>shared platform</strong> for the whole team: dashboards, process maps, requirements and change governance",
    "A vendor contract, support and onboarding",
    "A hosted model of the org that stays current without anyone running a refresh",
    "Business analysis and process documentation alongside the metadata",
  ]}
  notSfi={[
    "You need process maps, requirements traceability or a governance workflow. sf-intelligence has none of these",
    "People who won’t use an AI assistant or a terminal need the answers. There is no web app",
    "You need someone accountable for uptime and support. There is no SLA",
  ]}
  example={deleteField}
  sources={[
    { label: "elements.cloud home page", href: "https://elements.cloud/" },
    { label: "elements.cloud pricing", href: "https://elements.cloud/pricing/" },
  ]}
  verified={VERIFIED}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "Does Elements.cloud have an MCP server?",
      a: "Yes. Its home page leads with a deterministic metadata graph for Salesforce, available through MCP, and lists Claude Code, Cursor, Copilot and Agentforce. An earlier version of this page said it was planned; that is out of date.",
    },
    {
      q: "How much does Elements.cloud cost?",
      a: "Its pricing page lists Core Metadata Management from $6,800 a year for 100 Salesforce org users, rising by user tier. The Advanced plan and add-ons are quoted by their team. sf-intelligence is free.",
    },
    {
      q: "Can I use both?",
      a: "Yes. A team can keep Elements as its governance platform while individual admins and developers use sf-intelligence in their own assistant. Both speak MCP, so they can sit in the same client.",
    },
  ]}
>
  <h2>What’s actually different now</h2>
  <p>Until recently, “dependency answers inside your AI assistant” set sf-intelligence apart. Elements now offers that too, so it isn’t the reason to choose either. Three things still differ:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Price.</strong> Elements is priced by your number of Salesforce users. sf-intelligence costs nothing at any size.</li>
    <li><strong>Where the metadata lives.</strong> Elements builds its graph in its own service. sf-intelligence builds it on your computer and never uploads it.</li>
    <li><strong>Scope.</strong> Elements is a platform with process mapping and governance around the graph. sf-intelligence is only the question-answering part, and says plainly what it couldn’t check.</li>
  </ul>
</CompareLayout>
